    "md5": "0192387dd053602d447bfc530f7a690f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1973,
    "chunk_text": "Emergency help at your fingertips: Police introduce new SOS app\n\nColombo, Sep. 12 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka Police has introduced a new mobile application aimed at helping members of the public quickly notify police during emergencies. Police Media Spokesperson Assistant Superintendent of Police (ASP) F.U. Wootler said the \u201cSri Lanka Police\u201d app is currently available on the Google Play Store and can be used by Sri Lankan citizens who possess a National Identity Card (NIC).\n\nThe application includes an SOS emergency feature that allows users to request immediate police assistance. According to ASP Wootler, when the SOS button is continuously pressed for 10 seconds during an emergency, the user\u2019s location information will be transmitted to the nearest police station, enabling officers to respond more quickly.\n\nThe app provides an additional way of contacting the police alongside the existing 119 emergency hotline. Members of the public can use the application to alert police to emergencies, including situations where a child may be facing danger or distress.\n\nPolice have emphasized that the SOS function must not be used for testing, pranks or false emergency reports. Location sharing is mandatory when the SOS function is activated, and users are advised to enable the necessary permissions, particularly location access, in advance so the feature can operate effectively.\n\nThe new SOS service is currently being introduced as a pilot project in the Colombo District. Police have also warned the public against misusing emergency services, noting that legal action was taken against more than 91 individuals in 2025 for misusing the 119 emergency hotline.\n\nTo use the service, users must download and register through the \u201cSri Lanka Police\u201d app on the Google Play Store. Once opened, the home screen displays a red SOS button at the bottom centre, along with services including Police News, the Emergency Hotline, Station Finder and E-Services."
  },
