    "chunk_text": "The Indian Defence Minister\u2019s visit -- what happened  in-between\n\nWhat is working for the regime is its anti-corruption campaign\n\nOn\u00a0 September 8, Indian Defence Minister Ranjith Singh arrived in Colombo. It was the first visit of an Indian Defence Minister to our country in 38 years. It is also the first visit of an Indian Defence Minister to Sri Lanka since the controversial deployment of the Indian Peace Keeping Force (IPKF) during the late 1980s.\n\nThe Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP) at that time vociferously decried the deployment of Indian troops here and what it referred to as Indian interference in Sri Lanka\u2019s internal problems. It accused the then government of traitorously selling the country to India.\n\nThe JVP took up arms against the then government and even went so far as to call for the death of then president. Posters with the slogan \u201cJR Maramu\u201d (\u2018Let\u2019s Kill JR\u2019) were plastered countrywide. Our current President Anura Kumara Dissanayake was probably an active member of the JVP at that time.\n\nThis time around, President Dissanayake hosted the Indian Defence Minister and signed three key Memoranda of Understanding (MoUs) on strengthening defence co-operation between our two countries.\u00a0 However,\u00a0 the full text and legal clauses of the documents have not been published in full detail.\n\nAccording to EBSCO (a research organisation), an estimated 40,000 to 60,000 people were killed or went missing during the second JVP insurrection, which peaked fiercely in 1989. An exact tally however is very difficult to\u00a0 get; but human rights organisations generally agree on these figures."
  },
