    "md5": "ea046d845a16e3b90c51434f2936fdb4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1811,
    "chunk_text": "The efficacy of these last resort sanctions is yet to be seen. President Trump has called the new sanctions \u2018Economic D-Day One \u2019, invoking the Normandy landing of World War II.\u00a0 However, the success or failure of the latest strategy would be decided far from Washington or Tehran \u2014 and obviously not in Normandy \u2014 but in Beijing, which buys 80 per cent of Iranian oil at a premium discount. China has not heeded American calls, nor does the Trump administration, which habitually bullies its Western allies,\u00a0 seem to dare to antagonise the Middle Kingdom. Mr Trump recently announced tariffs on Canadian aircraft maker Bombardier, and accused Canada of treating America as a piggy bank.\n\nYet, the Islamic regime, which had weathered decades of economic sanctions and incurred an estimated $ 270\u00a0 billion economic cost of the recent war so far, has an unprecedentedly high pain threshold. After massacring 30,000 protesters in January and terrorising the population into submission, Ayatollahs and regime hardliners have little reason to fear another round of popular protests.\n\nAll that portends a protracted crisis: despite the Trump administration\u2019s claims of total control of the Strait of Hormuz, the last ten-day average of ships that had passed through the strait was 13, which is a fraction of the 100-140 ships that passed through the strait before the war. Despite the blockade, the oil price had not yet shot up to the highs of\u00a0 $120 March this year, at the beginning of the war.\u00a0 Ironically, the great stabiliser of the global oil price is neither OPEC nor America, but China, whose seaborn crude oil imports have declined by 40% since February. China\u2019s release of its large strategic reserves and the rapid shift to electrification have effectively reined in the runaway oil price."
  },
