    "chunk_size_bytes": 1994,
    "chunk_text": "All that portends a protracted crisis: despite the Trump administration\u2019s claims of total control of the Strait of Hormuz, the last ten-day average of ships that had passed through the strait was 13, which is a fraction of the 100-140 ships that passed through the strait before the war. Despite the blockade, the oil price had not yet shot up to the highs of\u00a0 $120 March this year, at the beginning of the war.\u00a0 Ironically, the great stabiliser of the global oil price is neither OPEC nor America, but China, whose seaborn crude oil imports have declined by 40% since February. China\u2019s release of its large strategic reserves and the rapid shift to electrification have effectively reined in the runaway oil price.\n\nInstead of public threats, the Trump administration has reportedly resorted to private channels to implore the countries to comply with its sanctions on Iran.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s trade with Iran is $81 million, heavily favouring Colombo.\n\nIran is Sri Lanka\u2019s 32nd export destination, and Sri Lanka\u2019s exports to Iran amounted to $76 million, comprising mainly tea in 2025. Sri Lanka\u2019s imported goods worth\u00a0 $ 5 million from Iran. While bilateral trade had reduced to a fraction in the aftermath of US sanctions, previously Iran supplied the majority of Sri Lanka\u2019s crude oil.\n\nColombo also currently bypasses the banking system through a tea-for-oil barter deal to pay a legacy crude oil debt of $ 250 million.\u00a0 Under this agreement, Sri Lanka ships $5million worth of tea every month to Iran. Earlier, at the outset of the Iran war in February, the government announced that Sri Lanka would continue to honour the barter agreement.\n\nThe latest American sanctions target a broader ecosystem that sustains the Islamic regime, and they may not have an immediate effect on Sri Lanka, given the limited volume. However, the government should keep an eye on the mood swings of the Trump administration, so as not to risk Sri Lanka\u2019s exports to its largest export market."
  },
