    "chunk_size_bytes": 1895,
    "chunk_text": "Magnitude 6.5 earthquake strikes near Jakarta\n\nThe malaysian Reserve- A magnitude 6.5 earthquake struck near Jakarta\u2019s Thousand Islands (Kepulauan Seribu) early Saturday, with tremors felt in the Indonesian capital and across parts of Java and Sumatra.\n\nMeteorology, Climatology and Geophysics Agency (BMKG) Earthquake and Tsunami Director Dr Wijayanto said the earthquake struck at 4.23am local time, with the magnitude revised from an initial 5.9 following further analysis.\n\nHe said its epicentre was in the Java Sea, 69 kilometres (km) northeast of Jakarta\u2019s Thousand Islands, at a depth of 368km.\n\n\"Taking into account the location of the epicentre and the depth of its hypocentre, the earthquake was a deep earthquake caused by intraslab activity,\u201d he said in a statement, adding that tsunami modelling showed no tsunami threat.\n\nThe earthquake came less than 10 hours after a magnitude 6.0 quake struck the Banda Sea in eastern Indonesia at 6.56pm on Friday at a depth of 148km, with its epicentre 162km northeast of Southwest Maluku and tremors felt across parts of Maluku.\n\nWijayanto said the strongest tremors from Saturday\u2019s earthquake were reported in parts of Banten and West Java, as well as Tanggamus in southern Sumatra, where the shaking was perceptible by people indoors and could cause windows and doors to rattle.\n\nHe said the tremors were also clearly felt indoors in Jakarta\u2019s Tanjung Priok and several other areas, while weaker shaking was reported as far away as Yogyakarta and Malang in Java, and Padang and the Mentawai Islands in western Sumatra.\n\nSome of strongest tremors were reportedly felt in Pandeglang in Banten, which lies along the Sunda Strait near the Anak Krakatau volcano which experienced renewed eruptive activity last week. However, Wijayanto said, Saturday\u2019s earthquake was tectonic in origin, with BMKG reporting no aftershocks as of 5am."
